import{c as e,Q as a,j as o,m as n}from"./chunks/framework.BXc4yaFE.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"My Week with Fable","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-09-@MatthewBerman-669176.md","filePath":"research/tweets/2026-06-09-@MatthewBerman-669176.md","lastUpdated":1785909234000}'),s={name:"research/tweets/2026-06-09-@MatthewBerman-669176.md"};function i(r,t,l,d,h,m){return a(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[n('<h1 id="my-week-with-fable" tabindex="-1">My Week with Fable <a class="header-anchor" href="#my-week-with-fable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;My Week with Fable&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>作者</strong>：Matthew Berman (@MatthewBerman)<br><strong>發布時間</strong>：2026-06-09<br><strong>原始連結</strong>：<a href="https://x.com/matthewberman/status/2064393462028669176" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://x.com/matthewberman/status/2064393462028669176</a><br><strong>類型</strong>：Twitter Article<br><strong>統計</strong>：❤️ 1,027 · 🔁 47 · 🔖 984 · 👁 169,019</p><hr><h2 id="分析摘要" tabindex="-1">分析摘要 <a class="header-anchor" href="#分析摘要" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;分析摘要&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>TL;DR</strong>：Fable/Mythos 一周個人測試：workflow mode（數百 agents 並行）、「長 horizon 任務是這個模型的主場」；Quirks：過度 verbose、喜歡確認問題、速度慢；最重要建議：effort level 調低，比你想象的更低。</p><h3 id="核心論點" tabindex="-1">核心論點 <a class="header-anchor" href="#核心論點" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;核心論點&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Workflow mode 亮點</strong>：「full code review」→ 對 application 幾乎每個檔案派一個 agent；找到 bugs、edge cases、缺少文件、UX 問題——其他模型同樣 prompt 找到的少得多</li><li><strong>高度自主性</strong>：比任何 Claude/GPT 模型都更願意離開工作數小時；「感覺每次啟動 Fable，它都有接手巨型項目的意圖」</li><li><strong>Speed is the caveat</strong>：比前代 Opus 和 GPT 慢（raw tokens/sec + 路徑選擇兩個維度）；5 分鐘才幾千 tokens；它想盡可能深思熟慮</li><li><strong>Effort level 關鍵建議</strong>：降低 effort，比你認為需要的還低；連 middle 設定都思考大量；即使在 low effort 仍然非常有能力</li><li><strong>Verbosity 是最大 quirk</strong>：信息密度高到讓作者感覺很蠢；clarifying questions 多（但 Anthropic 說 system prompt 更新後會修）；資訊密度高的論點：固定 token 預算下傳遞更多資訊 = 模型有效更智慧</li></ol><h3 id="批判性觀察" tabindex="-1">批判性觀察 <a class="header-anchor" href="#批判性觀察" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;批判性觀察&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li>個人體驗文章，無量化對比，可信度中等</li><li>「信息密度高是 token 效率的論點」是有趣的反向解讀，但未實際驗證</li><li>Effort level 建議（調低）與 @Mnilax 文章（xhigh 用於長任務）有矛盾——兩人測試場景不同</li></ol><hr><h2 id="評分" tabindex="-1">評分 <a class="header-anchor" href="#評分" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;評分&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>維度</th><th>分數</th><th>理由</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A. Workspace 可行動性</td><td>5/10</td><td>effort level 調低建議是可行動的；其餘多是觀察</td></tr><tr><td>B. 創新性</td><td>5/10</td><td>information density argument 有趣；其餘是典型 launch-day 感受文</td></tr><tr><td>C. 證據品質</td><td>5/10</td><td>個人測試，4 個 project；no quantitative comparison</td></tr><tr><td>D. 技術深度</td><td>5/10</td><td>workflow mode agent fleet 觀察；無技術細節</td></tr><tr><td>E. 泛化性</td><td>6/10</td><td>Fable 特定；effort level 建議 + 長 horizon framing 可泛化</td></tr><tr><td><strong>加權總分</strong></td><td><strong>5.15/10</strong></td><td>A×0.3 + B×0.2 + C×0.2 + D×0.15 + E×0.15</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>整合決策</strong>：不整合（&lt; 6 分，個人體驗為主）<br><strong>整合狀態</strong>：inbox/low-score</p><hr><h2 id="全文" tabindex="-1">全文 <a class="header-anchor" href="#全文" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;全文&quot;">​</a></h2><p>tl;dr I&#39;ve been testing Fable (Mythos) for the past week and it feels unlike any other model I&#39;ve used. It feels, and is priced, like a next-generation model. It also has some real quirks.</p><h2 id="the-good" tabindex="-1">The Good <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-good" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Good&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Workflow mode is the standout. I told it to do a &quot;full code review&quot; and watched it spin up hundreds of agents in parallel. It assigned an individual agent to basically every file in my application. It found bugs, edge cases, missing documentation, and UX improvements all over my application. And I&#39;ve given this same prompt to other models that didn&#39;t find nearly as many issues.</p><p>It&#39;s also wildly autonomous. It&#39;s way more willing than any previous Claude or GPT model to go off and work for hours at a time. Most of all, I trusted it to complete the goal I set. It&#39;ll happily burn a ton of tokens to get there.</p><p>I felt more confident in giving Fable massive, complex tasks. More so than any other model I&#39;ve ever used. I couldn&#39;t think of a problem that would make it stumble. And that&#39;s where this model stands out - long horizon tasks. It&#39;s hard to imagine where the limit of its time-horizon is.</p><p>It&#39;s not a God model though. There are things it needs to improve.</p><h2 id="quirks" tabindex="-1">Quirks <a class="header-anchor" href="#quirks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Quirks&quot;">​</a></h2><p>It&#39;s incredibly verbose. Explanations get deep in the weeds fast. I updated my claude.md to rein it in and even that wasn&#39;t enough. I kept having to ask it to explain things more simply. It wasn&#39;t just the verbosity either, it was the information density. The way it explained things made me feel genuinely dumb.</p><p>A note on information density - I didn&#39;t appreciate how important this is. The more information you can convey in a fixed token budget allows the model to be effectively smarter at a cheaper cost. This also is a strong argument for agents inventing their own hyper-dense language in the future.</p><p>Fable loves clarifying questions. A single prompt turns into: questions, then a summary of my answers, then confirm the summary, then a spec, then confirm the spec, then confirm the agent approach (parallel vs sequential), then finally build. I wanted it to make those decisions for me. Anthropic told me this gets fixed with the updated system prompt.</p><p>It feels slow. Slower than previous Opus models and even GPT. Slow to start, and it takes its time working through problems. That&#39;s the opposite of what I&#39;ve loved about Opus. Opus always felt faster than GPT-5.5 to me on two fronts: raw tokens/sec, and that it found shorter paths to solutions. Fable is different on both. Even on simple tasks it would crawl. I&#39;d watch the timer climb while the output tokens sat static, five minutes in and only a few thousand tokens used. It wants to be as thorough as possible, and that takes time.</p><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Pro tip: turn down the effort level, even more than you think you need to. It thinks a LOT even at the middle setting. Even on low effort it&#39;s incredibly capable and still thinks for a while.</p><p>All of these quirks are fixable - with model optimizations and increased compute capacity to increase speed, and with more fine-tuning/RL and system prompt adjustments to help with verbosity and being overly cautious to make decisions for me.</p><p>Verdict: MYTHOS is extremely capable, and I&#39;m still figuring out how to get the most out of it. It felt like it wanted my hardest tasks and anything less wasn&#39;t good enough. This is the first look at a brand new training run and it&#39;s already the most capable model I&#39;ve used.</p><p>That&#39;s the part I can&#39;t stop thinking about.</p>',31)])])}const c=e(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,c as default};
